WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.600
Collection and preservation determine how reliable the investigation can be.

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:16.980
Disk images, targeted collections, memory capture at a high level, log exports, EDR exports, registry hives, user profile artifacts, and cloud or domain records may all be useful.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:24.640
The right approach depends on incident urgency, system importance, data sensitivity, available tools, and legal or policy requirements.

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:29.340
Triage collection is often used when analysts need a focused snapshot quickly.

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:37.080
It can gather key logs, registry hives, event records, running process information, selected files, and system metadata.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:43.380
Triage is not the same as a full forensic image, so reports should explain what was collected and what may be missing.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:49.600
Memory capture may be valuable when volatile evidence matters, but it should be handled carefully and documented.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:53.420
Hashes help verify whether collected evidence changes after collection.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:01:00.280
Chain of custody records document who collected evidence, when it was collected, where it came from, where it was stored, and who handled it.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:07.860
Case notes should capture commands, tools, timestamps, observations, decisions, assumptions, and limitations.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.760
These records help distinguish analyst actions from system activity.

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:18.700
Reporting should include methods, evidence, findings, confidence, limitations, and supporting references.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:24.040
Windows artifacts can be complex, incomplete, or affected by normal system behavior.

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:27.380
A good report avoids overclaiming and explains uncertainty.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.080
The best outcome is not a dramatic story.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:36.140
It is a defensible explanation of what happened, when it happened, and what systems or users were affected.
